#!/usr/bin/env bash
# takeover-notice - tell a Claude chat that a blackout is freezing things, and
# how to get its own server back.
#
# Wired as a UserPromptSubmit hook, so every chat learns about a takeover the
# moment it is asked to do anything. Silent (and instant) when nothing is frozen.
#
# It exists because a frozen unit is invisible from inside a chat: it still
# reports "active", `systemctl start` on it silently does nothing, and only
# `restart`/`stop` admit anything is wrong. Without this note a chat sees a
# hanging localhost and goes off debugging an app that is working fine.
set -uo pipefail

FROZEN="$HOME/.cache/takeover/frozen"
LABEL_F="$HOME/.cache/takeover/label"
[ -s "$FROZEN" ] || exit 0

n=$(grep -c . "$FROZEN")
label=$(cat "$LABEL_F" 2>/dev/null)
units=$(grep . "$FROZEN" | head -14 | paste -sd'\n' | tr '\n' '@' | sed 's/@$//; s/@/, /g')
(( n > 14 )) && units="$units, +$(( n - 14 )) more"

cat <<EOF
<system-note>
A takeover/blackout is running on this laptop${label:+ (\"$label\")}. $n programs are FROZEN
with the cgroup freezer, not stopped: $units

A frozen unit still reports "active", and \`systemctl --user start\` on it succeeds
while doing nothing at all; restart and stop fail with "Cannot perform operation on
frozen unit". So if a localhost port, a testbed URL, \`dash\`, or the agent Chrome
hangs or refuses, it is frozen, not broken.

Take just what you need out of the freezer with \`takeover thaw <unit>\` (\`takeover
status\` lists them) and carry on. Do not debug it, do not kill it, and do not run
\`takeover finish\` unless you were asked to end the blackout.
</system-note>
EOF
